---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeFancyText from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeFancyText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="bold-text-generator">
  <DevEncodeFancyText
    client:load
    sample="Now hiring"
    featured={['bold', 'sans-bold', 'bold-italic', 'script-bold', 'fraktur-bold']}
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Bold where there is no bold button</h2>
    <p>
      LinkedIn posts, Instagram bios, Twitter/X, Facebook, most form fields and many internal tools
      store your text as plain text. They have no bold button, because there is no place to store the
      formatting. Unicode bold uses characters that are already drawn heavy, so the weight is part of
      the text.
    </p>

    <h2>Which bold to pick</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Style</th><th>Sample</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sans-serif bold</td><td>𝗛𝗲𝗮𝗱𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲</td><td>Social posts: it matches the interface font, so it looks deliberate</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Serif bold</td><td>𝐇𝐞𝐚𝐝𝐥𝐢𝐧𝐞</td><td>A more formal, print-like feel</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bold italic</td><td>𝑯𝒆𝒂𝒅𝒍𝒊𝒏𝒆</td><td>One emphasised phrase, not a whole line</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bold script</td><td>𝓗𝓮𝓪𝓭𝓵𝓲𝓷𝓮</td><td>Decorative names and profile flourishes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bold fraktur</td><td>𝕳𝖊𝖆𝖉𝖑𝖎𝖓𝖊</td><td>Gothic branding; hard to read at small sizes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Sans-serif bold is the correct default for almost all social platforms, because it has the same
      weight and shape as the UI text around it. Serif bold looks pasted in on a sans-serif feed.
    </p>

    <h2>Do numbers bold?</h2>
    <p>
      Only in some sets. Unicode has bold digits in the serif and sans-serif bold blocks, so
      &ldquo;50% off&rdquo; becomes fully bold there. The italic, script and fraktur blocks have no
      digits, so numbers stay at normal weight, which looks like a mistake. Check the preview before
      you post text with figures.
    </p>

    <h2>Use it sparingly, and here is why</h2>
    <p>
      Screen readers often read mathematical bold letters one at a time, so a fully bold post can be
      unusable for a blind reader. Search and hashtag indexing often skips these characters, so people
      may not find a bold keyword. Several platforms now rank posts lower when they use these
      characters heavily.
    </p>
    <p>
      Make one line bold (a headline, a job title, or one call to action) and keep the body in ordinary
      text. This keeps the emphasis, the accessibility and the search results.
    </p>

    <h2>LinkedIn specifics</h2>
    <p>
      Bold Unicode works in posts, comments, the headline field and the About section. It does not work
      in the name field, which LinkedIn validates, and it is against the LinkedIn name policy. A
      recruiter who searches for a job title does not find a bold one, so keep a plain-text version on
      the profile.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing leaves your browser</h2>
    <p>
      The bold text generator converts character codes as you type, in this tab. Drafts such as an
      announcement, a resignation post or a job ad that is not yet public stay on your device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
